            "Let me be honest first: I am not someone who has “won” against the phone. I grew up with no phone and no internet at home, and maybe that is why, when I finally got one, it felt like a whole new world in my hand. For years it was the biggest leak in my day.",
            "The pattern was always the same. A hard question, a small itch of discomfort, and my hand would go to the phone almost on its own. “Just checking the time.” Then one reel. Then a video of a topper's daily routine, which I told myself was “motivation”. Then a debate about some current issue, which I told myself was “current affairs”. Two hours later, I felt worse than before, tired without having worked.",
            "Cal Newport, in Deep Work, describes something called attention residue, a term from the researcher Sophie Leroy. When you switch from one task to another, part of your attention stays stuck on the first one. So even a quick look at the phone doesn't cost you just one minute. It leaves a little fog on the next half hour of study. That explained why, after “just one minute”, the chapter never felt sharp again.",
            "In Digital Minimalism, Newport says that apps are designed by very clever people to hold our attention, so it's not a fair fight if we only use willpower. That line made me stop blaming myself so much. It's not that I'm weak. It's that I was trying to resist something built to be hard to resist.",
            "What worked for me were small, boring changes. Atomic Habits calls it making the bad habit invisible and difficult. I started keeping the phone in another room while studying, or in a bag, switched off, on the far side of the room. I removed the reels app for exam months; I could still use it on a laptop if I really wanted to, and the extra steps were usually enough to stop me. I set the screen to grey in the evenings, which makes it surprisingly dull.",
