            "I have watched more topper interviews than I can count. For a while it was my evening ritual. Dinner, then a topper video “for motivation”. And night after night I went to bed feeling smaller. They woke at 4 a.m. They read the newspaper in 30 minutes. They cleared in their first attempt while also working a job. And I couldn't even finish my day's target.",
            "I kept watching because I hoped the next video would have the secret. One magic method that would fix everything. It never did. Instead, every video added one more thing I was “doing wrong”.",
            "Daniel Kahneman's Thinking, Fast and Slow helped me see one trap in this: we judge by the stories we see, not by all the stories that exist. In statistics this is called survivorship bias. We only hear from the few hundred who cleared, not from the lakhs who followed the same routine and didn't. So their routine looks like the reason for their success, even when luck, background and timing also played big parts.",
            "There's also how memory works in an interview. A topper looking back after success remembers the story neatly. The messy days, the doubts, the wasted weeks fade away. They are not lying. That's just how all of us remember. But we then compare our messy present with their neat, edited past.",
            "David Burns, in Feeling Good, lists the thinking errors that make us miserable. Two of them fitted me perfectly: “should statements”, like “I should wake at 4 like him”, and “labelling”, like “I am a failure because I don't”. Once I could name them, they lost some of their power.",
            "A senior once gave me a rule that I still follow: watch one topper interview for what you can use, write down at most one idea from it, and then switch it off and go study. If a video leaves me heavier than before, it was entertainment pretending to be motivation.",
