            "On my first day in Delhi, in August 2018, I was so scared that I skipped the freshers' party. I was a small-town boy from Rawatbhata in a huge city, and the city didn't know I existed. Later, in 2022, when I moved back to Delhi for Vajiram, I lived the aspirant's version of that loneliness: a small room, a bed, a table, a shelf of books, and a window that looked at another wall.",
            "Mornings were fine, because I was rushing for the 7:30 batch. Evenings were the hard part. Coming back to a silent room, eating alone, the same four walls. Some days I realised at night that I hadn't spoken a single full sentence to anyone except the shopkeeper. On festivals it was worst, hearing crackers and families outside while I sat with my notes.",
            "I thought feeling lonely meant something was wrong with me. That a serious aspirant should be happy alone with books. So I hid it, even from my parents on the phone. “Sab badhiya hai, Mummy.”",
            "Vivek Murthy, who served as the Surgeon General of the United States, wrote a whole book on this called Together. He says loneliness is a signal, like hunger or thirst. Hunger tells you the body needs food; loneliness tells you that you need people. It's not weakness. It's a basic human need asking to be met. He also says loneliness is far more common than people admit, because everyone hides it, just like I did.",
            "Things got better in small ways. I made friends with two other aspirants in my lane, and we started having tea together every evening at the same tapri, ten minutes, no study talk allowed. Those ten minutes were sometimes the best part of my day. I started calling home properly, not just “sab badhiya hai”, but actually telling my mother when the day had been bad. And once a week I went to a park or a library, just to be among people.",
            "Kristin Neff's idea of common humanity also helped: every lonely aspirant thinks he is the only lonely one, which is exactly what makes it so lonely. When we say it out loud, it becomes a shared thing instead of a private shame.",
